course: dictionary
lesson: 14576
title: The pipette
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A pipette is a thin glass or plastic tube. Scientists use it to measure or move very small amounts of liquid, drop by drop. When a scientist needs an exact amount, a pipette is the tool to use, not a spoon or a cup.
explain: To use a pipette, squeeze the bulb at the top, dip the tip into the liquid, then let go. The liquid is sucked up into the tube. Squeeze gently to let out the drops, one at a time.
explain: «Use the pipette to add three drops of water.» Here pipette is a noun, the tool itself. «Pipette two drops of indicator into the tube.» Here pipette is a verb, which means to move liquid with a pipette. Check the words around it to see which one it is.
fact: A pipette is a noun: a thin glass or plastic tube.
fact: «Pipette» can also be a verb, meaning to move liquid with a pipette.
fact: A pipette moves small amounts of liquid, drop by drop.
fact: Another name for a pipette is a dropper.
fact: Measuring carefully with a pipette helps to make a fair test.
exercise: «Ravi used a pipette to add three drops of water to the test tube.» What is a pipette? | *A thin glass or plastic tube for moving small amounts of liquid | A large bowl for mixing paint | A heavy metal stand for holding test tubes
exercise: «First squeeze the bulb, then dip the pipette into the liquid.» What do you do to suck up the liquid? | Blow into the tube with your mouth | *Squeeze the bulb, dip the tip, then let go | Shake the tube hard
exercise: Mia says: «I pipetted the liquid drop by drop.» What does pipetted mean? | To freeze the liquid solid | *To move liquid with a pipette | To pour all the liquid out in one big splash | To paint a picture
test: Why do scientists use a pipette in a fair test? | *So they add exact, accurate amounts of liquid | To make the lab look tidy | To stop the liquid from moving | To make the liquid colder
test: «Kim gently squeezed the pipette to let out one drop.» What does gently suggest? | *Softly, so only a small amount comes out | Very fast, so the liquid splashes everywhere | Hard enough to break the tube
exam: Which sentence uses pipette as a verb? | «She put the pipette back in the box.» | *«Pipette two drops into the dish.» | «The science lab has twelve benches.» | «The pipette was made of glass.»
